==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|kneo}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*knewą}} (compare Low German {{m|nds|Knee}}, Dutch {{m|nl|knie}}, English {{m|en|knee}}, Danish {{m|da|knæ}}, Swedish {{m|sv|knä}}), from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*ǵnéwo-}}, a thematic derivative of {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*ǵónu}}. Compare {{etyl|hit|-}} {{m|hit|𒄀𒉡|tr=genu|sc=Xsux}}, Latin {{m|la|genū}}, {{etyl|xto|-}} {{m|xto|kanweṃ}} (dual), {{etyl|txb|-}} {{m|txb|kenī}}, Ancient Greek {{m|grc|γόνυ||knee}}, {{m|grc|γωνία||corner, angle}}, Old Armenian {{m|xcl|ծունր|}}, Avestan {{m|ae|𐬲𐬥𐬎𐬨||tr=žnum|sc=Avst}}, Sanskrit {{m|sa|जानु|tr=jānu}}.

